From a9e016c920dcd8733bb462ceb8d7ffa21c42c177 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 12:21:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] vulkan/wsi: Horizon, sleep between dequeue attempts instead
 of spinning
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

mesa-nvk-horizon: Phase 6 item 7 (nwindowDequeueBuffer / nwindowQueueBuffer)
Why: the acquire was starving the compositor it was waiting for.

 eventWait cannot wait on this window. The release event is permanently
 signalled — measured, 84327 returns in five seconds, 59 us apiece — so
 a loop that "waits" on it has no idle in it, and what is left is binder
 transactions as fast as the CPU can issue them.

 t_nwindow's copy of this loop was made to report itself, and the
 numbers are unambiguous: 8320 rounds in one second, both dequeue modes
 reached, both answering NO_INIT, and 989 ms of the 1000 ms budget spent
 inside bqDequeueBuffer — roughly 17000 transactions a second into the
 compositor's own service. Three lines later in the same log, after
 nothing but a log line being written to the SD card, async=true
 returned a buffer in 146 us on its first attempt. A three-second budget
 changes nothing: 23192 rounds, same NO_INIT. More asking never helps
 and stopping always does.

 Three images never showed it because a slot is nearly always free and
 this loop never spins. Two images spin on every frame.

What: the retry waits with svcSleepThread for an eighth of a refresh
 instead of calling eventWait on an event that returns immediately.
 Nothing against a 16.7 ms frame, and enough to leave the compositor
 alone. The caller's deadline is still checked at the top of the loop,
 so vkAcquireNextImageKHR(timeout = 0) answers VK_NOT_READY without ever
 reaching the sleep.

 This supersedes 0062's slicing, which dressed a spin up as a wait and
 is what hid this for five hardware runs.

Cross build: green. Not verified on hardware.
---
 src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c | 54 +++++++++++++++++++-----------------
 1 file changed, 29 insertions(+), 25 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c b/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c
index 7f68685..7d8e83a 100644
--- a/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c
+++ b/src/vulkan/wsi/wsi_horizon.c
@@ -1159,41 +1159,45 @@ wsi_horizon_acquire_zero_copy(struct wsi_horizon_swapchain *chain,
          return VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DATE_KHR;
       }
 
-      /* THE WAIT IS SLICED, AND THAT IS THE FIX.
+      /* THE WAIT IS A REAL SLEEP, AND THAT IS THE FIX.
        *
-       * This used to spend the whole remaining timeout inside one
-       * eventWait and then retry in async=false. On hardware every
-       * two-image swapchain died there: the acquire waited, the event
-       * fired, the single async=false attempt that followed failed,
-       * and the deadline was gone before async=true was ever asked
-       * again. t_nwindow's own copy of the loop reported the same
-       * failure as LibnxError_Timeout — its own budget expiring, not
-       * anything the BufferQueue said.
+       * eventWait cannot wait on this window. The release event is
+       * permanently signalled — measured on hardware, 84327 returns in
+       * five seconds, 59 us apiece — so a loop that "waits" on it has
+       * no idle in it at all, and what is left is binder transactions
+       * as fast as the CPU can issue them.
        *
-       * Two refreshes per slice: a release that does not come costs
-       * one slice and another attempt, not the entire acquire. A
-       * timeout from eventWait is therefore not a failure here; it is
-       * the reason to ask again, and the caller's deadline is checked
-       * at the top of the loop where it belongs.
+       * That is what was wrong, and the number is unambiguous.
+       * t_nwindow's copy of this loop reported itself: 8320 rounds in
+       * one second, both dequeue modes reached, both answering NO_INIT,
+       * and **989 ms of the 1000 ms budget spent inside
+       * bqDequeueBuffer** — roughly 17000 transactions a second into
+       * the compositor's own service. Three lines later, after nothing
+       * but a log line being written, async=true returned a buffer in
+       * 146 us on its first attempt. A three-second budget changes
+       * nothing: 23192 rounds, same answer. More asking never helps and
+       * stopping always does.
+       *
+       * So the producer was starving the consumer it was waiting for.
+       * Three images never showed it because a slot is nearly always
+       * free and this loop never spins.
+       *
+       * An eighth of a refresh: nothing against a 16.7 ms frame, and
+       * enough to leave the compositor alone. The caller's deadline is
+       * checked at the top of the loop, so a zero timeout still answers
+       * VK_NOT_READY without ever reaching here.
        */
-      const uint64_t slice_ns = 2 * WSI_HORIZON_REFRESH_NS;
-      uint64_t left_ns = slice_ns;
+      uint64_t sleep_ns = WSI_HORIZON_REFRESH_NS / 8;
       if (timeout_ns != UINT64_MAX) {
          const uint64_t spent_ns = os_time_get_nano() - start_ns;
          if (spent_ns >= timeout_ns)
             return VK_TIMEOUT;
          const uint64_t budget_ns = timeout_ns - spent_ns;
-         if (budget_ns < left_ns)
-            left_ns = budget_ns;
+         if (budget_ns < sleep_ns)
+            sleep_ns = budget_ns;
       }
 
-      Result erc = eventWait(&chain->window->event, left_ns);
-      if (R_FAILED(erc) &&
-          R_VALUE(erc) != MAKERESULT(Module_Kernel, KernelError_TimedOut)) {
-         mesa_loge("wsi_horizon: waiting for a buffer release failed: "
-                   "0x%08x", erc);
-         return VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_DATE_KHR;
-      }
+      svcSleepThread(sleep_ns);
    }
 }
 
